BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:rBxxeqJRlJ6sRYqzJIqBQ
SUMMARY:Essays on Market Design\, Complementarities\, and Technology Adopti
	on
DTSTAMP:20260716T055639Z
DTSTART:20260720T150000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Jingyun Huang  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Def
	ense  Department: Economics  This dissertation studies how allocation is s
	haped by complementarities\, participation frictions\, and the design of m
	arket institutions. The chapters examine settings in which agents make cho
	ices over related objects or technologies: highway contracts sold sequenti
	ally within a day\, spectrum licenses sold through a simultaneous clock au
	ction\, and consumer-facing generative AI tools adopted across countries. 
	Across these settings\, the dissertation asks how observed choices reveal 
	economic primitives\, and how market outcomes change when agents face cons
	traints on entry\, capacity\, information\, or access.  The first chapter 
	studies sequential highway procurement auctions in Oklahoma. Morning and a
	fternoon contracts are sold on the same day\, allowing early awards to aff
	ect later entry and bidding. I estimate a structural model that separates 
	standalone costs\, cost synergies\, fixed entry costs\, and firm-day capac
	ity constraints. The estimates show that small firms obtain larger proport
	ional cost savings from same-day project synergies\, but higher participat
	ion barriers limit their ability to act on these gains. Counterfactuals co
	mparing sequential auctions\, simultaneous unbundled auctions\, and target
	ed bundling show that auction format affects both procurement spending and
	 the distribution of awards across firms.  The second chapter studies FCC 
	Auction 110\, a 5G spectrum auction in which bidders may value combination
	s of geographic markets. I use a revealed-preference approach to construct
	 moment inequalities from clock-phase bidding choices and recover bounds o
	n standalone values and geographic complementarities. The estimates suppor
	t positive complementarities across markets. Counterfactual reallocations 
	indicate that locally supported changes can raise value\, though the welfa
	re conclusions remain bounded rather than point identified.  The third cha
	pter measures global adoption of consumer-facing generative AI tools using
	 high-frequency website traffic data from Semrush. The chapter documents r
	apid entry\, persistent ChatGPT dominance\, accelerating usage\, widening 
	adoption gaps between high- and low-income countries\, and strong home-mar
	ket advantages for non-U.S. tools. Together\, the chapters show that alloc
	ation in modern markets depends not only on preferences and costs\, but al
	so on the institutional and technological conditions that determine who ca
